<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Craig Roberts - Full Pack Part 1</title></head><body><h1>Craig Roberts - Full Pack Part 1</h1><p>Craig Roberts — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 1)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>03-04 Performance Review</p><p>Plaud file id: de3cd31f775c8565479b3e18a7780ab3</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance and Strategy Review: Craig Roberts</p><p>In this comprehensive performance review, Bob Duncan analyses Craig Roberts&#x27; recent sales metrics, highlighting a strong recovery in lead generation and written business while identifying significant opportunities for growth in protection sales and admin fee consistency. The discussion focusses on strategic improvements in client engagement through content creation, refining document collection workflows to prevent delays, and adopting a more assertive approach to fee charging, culminating in a clear set of actions to drive future revenue.</p><p>## Review of Past Actions and Current Workflow Status</p><p>The meeting commenced with a review of outstanding actions from the previous week, following a brief discussion about technical difficulties Craig was experiencing with his microphone. Bob Duncan led the review, confirming that Craig is up to date with administrative tasks, including having all necessary documents signed. A primary focus of the previous week&#x27;s action plan was the correction of appointment statuses—specifically ensuring that appointments listed as &#x27;booked&#x27; accurately reflected whether they had taken place or not.</p><p>Bob highlighted that Craig was tasked with clearing his workflow, stopping the habit of rolling dates forward, and addressing 31 clients who were previously listed as looking for property. Craig confirmed that he has been actively processing these clients, either by contacting them to progress their journey or moving them back to Sean McLennan for nurturing. The team noted that they have been &quot;blasting&quot; through these leads, indicating a high level of activity. Bob emphasised the importance of maintaining this momentum and ensuring that client statuses are accurate to prevent a backlog.</p><p>## Analysis of Lead Generation and Written Business</p><p>Bob provided a data-driven analysis of Craig&#x27;s lead generation and written business performance. The previous month saw 32 new leads, a positive upward trend compared to 30 in January, 24 in December, and 35 in November. The breakdown of these leads showed a mix of outcomes, including one at &#x27;depth five&#x27;, five marked as &#x27;Might Purchase With&#x27; (MPW), and six appointments still showing as booked from February, which Bob requested Sean to investigate. Despite the positive lead volume, Bob noted an unusual number of &quot;not purchasing&quot; outcomes and encouraged a review of why qualified leads might not be proceeding.</p><p>Craig reported that while the start of the week was slow, activity has picked up significantly, with three appointments booked on Monday and several more since. He expressed confidence in his pipeline, noting new mortgage cases in progress, including two due that day and one requiring booking. He also highlighted upcoming protection business, with a &quot;protection first&quot; appointment booked and a sign-up scheduled for Friday.</p><p>Financially, Craig is on track to nearly hit £10k in written business for February. However, Bob pointed out that a substantial performance is needed in March and April to reach the target of £45k. The current pipeline includes four cases in underwriting, with specific updates provided for clients Ross Gibson, Diane Morton, Emma McLaughlin, and Christopher Patterson. Craig noted the competitive nature of the West End market, where properties are selling rapidly, often before viewings can be arranged, which complicates the buying process for his clients.</p><p>## Deep Dive into Protection and Admin Fee Performance</p><p>The discussion shifted to a granular analysis of two critical revenue streams: protection insurance and administration fees. Bob noted a recovery in Craig&#x27;s protection stats, with Q3 insurance penetration rising to 36.29% from a low in the previous quarter. Current Q4 statistics show 7 applications and 3 insurance cases, placing Craig&#x27;s protection conversion at roughly 70% for the current period. Bob set a strategic challenge for Craig to aim for a 50% conversion rate by year-end, calculating that achieving just one extra protection case per week (9 total by quarter-end) could yield over £21,000 in additional revenue annually.</p><p>A significant portion of the review focussed on Administration Fees. Bob presented a benchmark comparison showing Craig&#x27;s year-to-date average fee percentage at 23%, significantly lower than the business average of 53%. This discrepancy represents a potential £20,000 swing in income compared to top performers like Claire (£24,500) and Paul (£16,000). Craig explained his hesitation often stems from finding out clients&#x27; financial constraints or personal connections. Bob and Sean coached Craig on this, suggesting he introduce the fee structure at the very beginning of the consultation to filter out transactional customers and establish value early. Bob argued that clients worried about a £500 fee in the context of a property purchase are likely underfunded or need to adjust their expectations, and waiving fees represents &quot;money you haven&#x27;t got.&quot;</p><p>## Strategic Discussion on Client Engagement and Process Improvement</p><p>The team discussed strategies to enhance client engagement and streamline internal processes. leveraging Craig&#x27;s insights into the fast-paced West End property market, Bob suggested Craig write a blog post. This content would detail the challenges buyers face—such as properties selling before Sunday viewings—and offer tips like submitting early, strong offers. This initiative aims to position Craig as a knowledgeable local expert and keep the brand &quot;Retty&quot; visible to clients, even those who may be shopping around.</p><p>On the operational side, Sean raised a point from the &quot;M and P checklist&quot; regarding document collection. Both Sean and Craig acknowledged that there has been too much back-and-forth with clients to gather necessary paperwork. To resolve this, they agreed on a stricter protocol where applications will not be submitted until approximately 99.9% of documents are in hand. They also established a workflow where they will explicitly agree on a case-by-case basis who is responsible for chasing specific outstanding items, ensuring clarity and compliance.</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Craig Roberts**</p><p>- [ ] Maintain workflows by keeping in touch with clients to secure purchases for the current month, next month, and beyond - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Draft a blog post about the West End market, highlighting its vibrancy and buyer challenges (e.g., need for early offers) - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Send the blog draft to marketing for finalisation and distribution - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Aim to achieve nine extra protection cases between now and the end of the quarter (approx. one per week) - [31/03/2024]</p><p>- [ ] Implement a strategy to state fee structure at the start of client conversations - [Immediate]</p><p>- [ ] Increase admin fee consistency, aiming to raise the average from 23% to closer to 50% over the next couple of months - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure all necessary documents are collected prior to submitting applications to eliminate chasing - [Immediate]</p><p>- [ ] Coordinate with Sean on a case-by-case basis to define who chases specific documents - [Immediate]</p><p>- [ ] Get the reports done for Ross Gibson and Diane Morton&#x27;s cases - [TBD]</p><p>**@Bob Duncan**</p><p>- [ ] Get/Update a projected exchange date for Ross Gibson and Diane Morton&#x27;s cases - [TBD]</p><p>**@Sean McLennan**</p><p>- [ ] Review the six appointments from February that are still showing as &#x27;booked&#x27; - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Investigate the three randomly created leads to see if they are test cases or system errors - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure all necessary documents are collected prior to submitting applications to eliminate chasing - [Immediate]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Internal Performance Review — Craig Roberts</p><p>**Creation date:** 2026-03-04 12:32:14</p><p>**Participants referenced:** Bob Duncan, Sean McLennan, Craig Roberts ---</p><p>## 1. Follow-Up From Previous Meeting</p><p>### Current Position (actions set last week)</p><p>- **Correct CRM “Appointment Booked” entries** to reflect whether appointments took place; stop **rolling workflow dates forward** without genuine contact/progress.</p><p>- **Review 31 clients** marked as *looking for a property* to confirm whether properties have been found / next steps.</p><p>### Vs Last Week</p><p>- **Workflows:** **0 overdue tasks** showing in workflow view (improvement vs last week’s concern about dates being rolled forward).</p><p>- **Appointments status hygiene:** Still **6 “Appointment Booked” leads from February** showing in CRM (not fully corrected).</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- **Workflow backlog control:** no overdue workflow tasks visible.</p><p>- **Behaviour change reported:** Craig stated he is **calling clients / taking actions**, and in some cases **moving items back to Sean**, rather than simply moving workflow dates forward.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Repeated CRM status issue:** **6 February leads** remain at **“Appointment Booked”** (may be March appointments, but not confirmed/updated in CRM).</p><p>- **Unclear lead creation/ownership:** **3 leads** marked **“Randomly Created”** remain present.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Reporting distortion:** unresolved February “Appointment Booked” outcomes distort conversion and capacity reporting.</p><p>- **Evidence gap:** calls/actions cannot be evidenced if **CRM notes/statuses/next steps** are not updated.</p><p>- **Purchase drift:** the **31 “looking for property”** segment can silently stall (Craig noted clients often lose properties before viewings/offers).</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Validate and update the **6 February “Appointment Booked”** entries (confirm: booked for March vs completed/cancelled/NPW).</p><p>- Investigate and clean up the **3 “Randomly Created”** leads (confirm if **B&amp;C pre-populates/tests**; correct owner/status or remove).</p><p>- Review the **31 property-search clients** and log a clear next step for each.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig):** review 31 property-search clients; ensure notes/updates reflect actual contact attempts and next steps.</p><p>- **Other (Sean):** resolve the 6 February appointment-booked records + 3 randomly created leads (as agreed in review).</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving:** workflow hygiene (0 overdue tasks) and reduced “date-rolling” behaviour.</p><p>- **Stagnant:** CRM data quality gaps (February appointment statuses + “randomly created” leads) remain unresolved.</p><p>---</p><p>## 2. Leads Performance Review (with CRM Discipline)</p><p>### A. Leads — Last Month (February)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **Total new leads:** **32**</p><p>- **CRM status issues impacting reporting:**</p><p>  - **6** = **Appointment Booked** (still sitting from February)</p><p>  - **3** = **Randomly Created**</p><p>- Outcomes referenced on screen included **Appointment Completed**, **Keeping in Contact**, **Not Proceeding** (reasons mentioned: **Not Interested**, **Not Purchasing**, **Unable to Contact**).</p><p>- Management observation: with **32 leads** and only **~5 Not Proceeding/NPW** referenced verbally, expectation is that most are “in touch” — but CRM needs to evidence this with **statuses/notes/next steps**.</p><p>#### Vs Last Month</p><p>- Not available (no January conversion/outcome breakdown provided).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- **Lead volume:** **32** in February (directionally improved vs December volume discussed elsewhere).</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Status hygiene failure:** **6 February leads** remain as **Appointment Booked** with no confirmed outcome recorded.</p><p>- **Process/system noise:** **3 “Randomly Created”** entries create ownership and reporting ambiguity.</p><p>- **Qualification leakage indicator:** presence of **Not Purchasing** as an outcome suggests weak pre-qualification and/or miscategorisation.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Hidden pipeline leakage:** incorrect statuses can mask uncontacted/lost cases and inflate engagement.</p><p>- **Follow-up audit weakness:** “Unable to contact” losses cannot be audited without logged cadence (tasks/notes/next steps).</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- For each of the **6 February “Appointment Booked”** leads: confirm appointment date/outcome and update **CRM status + notes**.</p><p>- For the **3 “Randomly Created”** leads: confirm origin and correct/close/remove to protect reporting accuracy.</p><p>- For actively worked leads assumed “in touch”: ensure each has **recent notes** and a **clear next step** logged.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Other (Sean):** resolve the 6 February appointment-booked records + 3 randomly created leads.</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig):** maintain accurate lead notes/statuses and ensure next steps exist on worked leads.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant:** lead volume is acceptable, but CRM discipline issues prevent reliable conversion and engagement reporting.</p><p>---</p><p>## 4. Applications in Progress</p><p>### Case: Ross Gibson &amp; Diane Morton</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Stage: **signed up**; **reports not yet completed**.</p><p>- CRM gap: **projected exchange date missing**.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Case is in active processing (not stalled at enquiry).</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Missing projected exchange date** reduces forecasting accuracy.</p><p>- **Reports outstanding** are blocking progress.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Forecasting/reporting inaccuracy and potential slippage if reports are delayed.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Add **projected exchange date** in CRM (or confirm unknown and log the action to obtain it).</p><p>- Complete outstanding **reports**.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)** + **Other (Sean)** (both referenced as involved).</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant:** progress is constrained by internal completion steps (reports + missing forecast field).</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: Emma McLaughlin</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Missing requirement: **breakdown of deposit source**.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- The exact blocker is clearly identified.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Case is paused pending client evidence; CRM must show what is outstanding and the return point.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Underwriting delay until deposit sourcing evidence is supplied.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Obtain and upload a clear **deposit breakdown**; update CRM notes with what’s outstanding.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant:** awaiting client documentation.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: Christopher Patterson (NatWest)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Lender: **NatWest**</p><p>- Stage: shown as **offer ready for offer document**, but offer document not issued due to **home report refresh** returning to lender.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Home report refresh completed and returned to NatWest.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Offer document not yet issued; timing uncertainty remains.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Offer issuance delay increases quarter slippage risk.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Chase NatWest for the offer document; log chase + next chase date in CRM notes.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving:** prerequisites appear complete; awaiting lender output.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: Unnamed client (offer accepted; “shopping around”)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Client re-engaged and confirmed **offer accepted**; Craig planned to “do his mortgage” on **2026-03-04**.</p><p>- Competitive risk: client has **5 other agents out**.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Soft-touch follow-up led to re-engagement and same-day intent to proceed.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Client not named** in the review summary; lender/stage (DIP vs application), doc position and ownership were not stated.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- High fallout risk due to multi-agent shopping; weak CRM visibility increases missed chasers and handoff gaps.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Update CRM with **client name**, stage (DIP/application), next action date, and document-chasing ownership.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant (evidence gap):** positive activity reported, but insufficient CRM detail to evidence control.</p><p>---</p><p>## 5. Protection in Progress</p><p>### Protection activity (dated)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **2026-03-04:** 2 mortgages progressing; **2 expected to include protection**.</p><p>- **2026-03-04:** **Protection First** booked.</p><p>- **2026-03-06:** **Protection sign-up** planned.</p><p>- **2026-03-05:** possible additional Protection First (not confirmed).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Time-bound protection activity stated with near-term intent (Protection First + sign-up dates).</p><p>- Protection expected to attach to **2** progressing mortgage cases.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Booking certainty gap for **2026-03-05** (“thought I had another…”), suggesting potential booking/CRM logging weakness.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Protection slippage if appointments/outcomes aren’t confirmed and logged.</p><p>- Cross-sell leakage if mortgages progress without clearly recorded protection discussion/next step.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Confirm whether a **Protection First** is booked for **2026-03-05** and ensure CRM notes + next step are recorded.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving:** protection activity is being scheduled alongside mortgage progression, but CRM certainty needs tightening.</p><p>---</p><p>### Protection underwriting pipeline / target cadence</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Protection pipeline: **3 cases** showing as **pipeline/on risk/awaiting underwriting**.</p><p>- One underwriting item has been present since **June** (long-running delay).</p><p>- Premium performance described as **well above** MAB average (**75**).</p><p>- Target discussion: aim to get as close to **50%** by year end; requirement framed as **9 extra cases** (approx **+1 case per week**).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- “Same one from last week” referenced for an underwriting pipeline item (no movement).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Premium quality above benchmark; Q4 start referenced as **7 mortgage apps / 3 protection**.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Long-running underwriting case (since June) remains unresolved and slows “on risk” timing.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Underwriting inertia delays protection recognition and may reduce realised conversion.</p><p>- Falling behind the **+1/week** rhythm risks missing the “50%” stretch.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Maintain **+1 additional protection case per week** cadence.</p><p>- Sean to help push underwriting to get cases **on risk sooner** where possible.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig):** booking/cadence and conversion.</p><p>- **Other (Sean):** underwriting progression support.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (activity/quality), but constrained:** strong premium quality and cadence intent, held back by underwriting lag.</p><p>---</p><p>## 6. Written Business — This Month (February)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Craig’s stated aim: **over £10k** for February; described as “pretty much almost there” (exact figure not stated in that discussion).</p><p>- Separate banking discussion referenced **“over £10.5k in February”** (context: quieter than January).</p><p>### Vs Last Month / Last Year</p><p>- Not available (no month-on-month or year-on-year written figures provided).</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Pipeline activity referenced (mortgages progressing and protection activity scheduled).</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Quarter/period pressure: discussion referenced needing to ramp and avoid over-reliance on later months to catch up.</p><p>- Purchase conversion bottleneck: clients struggling to secure offers accepted (West End competitiveness) delays writing.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Timing risk if “still looking” purchase clients do not secure properties soon; written business may slip.</p><p>- Without disciplined CRM progression, slippage will not be visible early enough to intervene.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Tighten progression for active buyers once a property is identified (offer support, DIP readiness, lender prep).</p><p>- Ensure every active case has clear next steps and ownership to prevent drift.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving but fragile:** momentum referenced, but dependent on market conditions and follow-up discipline.</p><p>---</p><p>## 7. Banked Business — This Month (February)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Banking pattern described as inverted vs peers: **strong January**, **quieter February**.</p><p>- February banking referenced as **over £10.5k**.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not provided (no comparable figures).</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Strong January performance relative to peers (as discussed).</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- February quieter than expected, increasing reliance on March/early-April conversion to influence quarter position.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Quarter-end slippage if March/early-April cases do not land in time to bank within the quarter.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Maintain writing momentum through March/early April to influence quarter banking.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant:** clear risk acknowledged; no supporting weekly banking trajectory provided.</p><p>---</p><p>## 10. Projected Income</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- **£11k projected** to drop between 2026-03-04 and quarter end (components referenced: **£8.3k** plus remainder to **£11k**).</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not provided.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Pipeline visibility exists with a quantified projected figure.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Reliance on timely case progression (missing exchange dates, missing docs, lender offer delays).</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Operational delay could push income beyond quarter end (exchange timing unknown, document gaps, lender timelines).</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Execute the actions under **Applications in Progress** to reduce slippage risk.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**; **Other (Sean)** where underwriting/admin support is required.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (identified), but execution-dependent:** income is identified; timing depends on removing known blockers.</p><p>---</p><p>## Fees / Revenue Uplift (Admin fee discipline)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Admin fee capture is low vs peers:</p><p>  - Craig: **£5k** year-to-date</p><p>  - Peers referenced: **Claire £24.5k**, **Paul £16k**, **Bob £11k**, **Karen £10k**</p><p>- Benchmark referenced: business **53%** vs Craig **23%** (year-to-date snapshot).</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not provided.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Craig states fees are raised at the start of the initial call/pitch; some cases include fees.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Discounting/waiving fees for friends/known contacts and when clients mention alternatives.</p><p>- Behaviour appears variable rather than consistent and front-loaded.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Material income leakage (described as a “£20k swing” vs top performer).</p><p>- Structural profitability risk if not corrected.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Standardise fee conversation at the very start (“This is my fee structure / what you get”).</p><p>- Ask explicitly: **“Are you happy with the fee?”**</p><p>- If challenged, quantify value and clarify what “cheaper elsewhere” means rather than defaulting to free.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Declining (relative to peers):** material gap vs benchmarks; improvement requires consistent behavioural change.</p><p>---</p><p>## Follow-up / Marketing Output (Client Engagement)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Craig to produce a **blog**: tips for buying in the **West End** market.</p><p>- TikTok option referenced (previously discussed before Christmas).</p><p>- Marketing can finalise/distribute once Craig provides content.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not provided.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Strong, practical market commentary already articulated and suitable for client-facing content.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Craig has “never written a blog before” which may slow delivery without a clear deadline.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Missed engagement opportunity with West End database; reliance on passive lead flow.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Draft key bullet points/outline and send to marketing.</p><p>- Confirm whether TikTok will be produced alongside the blog.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**; **Other (Marketing)** to format/distribute once draft received.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant:** value is clear, but delivery not yet evidenced.</p><p>---</p><p>## 11. Overall Performance Diagnosis</p><p>### Adviser strengths this week</p><p>- **Workflow engagement improving** (0 overdue tasks; less date-rolling reported).</p><p>- **Protection activity scheduled** with near-term sign-up intent and premium quality above benchmark.</p><p>- Strong West End market knowledge that can be reused for lead generation.</p><p>### Main risks</p><p>- **CRM/reporting hygiene gaps:** 6 February “Appointment Booked” leads unresolved; 3 “Randomly Created” leads unresolved; missing projected exchange dates (Ross Gibson &amp; Diane Morton); unnamed “offer accepted” case not trackable.</p><p>- **Case progression friction:** deposit evidence outstanding (Emma McLaughlin); offer document timing (Christopher Patterson, NatWest).</p><p>- **Revenue leakage:** admin fee performance materially behind peers (23% vs 53%; £5k vs peers up to £24.5k).</p><p>### Behaviour patterns / accountability concerns</p><p>- Positive: increased real contact activity reported rather than workflow date manipulation.</p><p>- Concern: repeated CRM accuracy gaps and incomplete case identification in reviews reduce control and auditability.</p><p>### Where management support is required</p><p>- **Sean:** resolve CRM noise items (6 appointment-booked; 3 randomly created) and push underwriting where possible.</p><p>- **Marketing:** convert Craig’s blog outline into publishable output once received.</p><p>---</p><p>## 12. Master Action List</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Check and correct the **6 February “Appointment Booked”** leads (confirm if booked into March; otherwise update outcome/status + notes) | Leads (February) | **Sean (Other)** | **2026-03-06** | Misreported activity/conversion; hidden uncontacted/lost leads; planning errors |</p><p>| Investigate and resolve the **3 “Randomly Created”** leads (confirm B&amp;C pre-populate/test vs genuine; correct owner/status or remove) | Leads (February) | **Sean (Other)** | **2026-03-06** | CRM/reporting pollution; unclear ownership; duplicated pipeline |</p><p>| Review the **31 “looking for property”** clients: confirm who has found a property, who needs chasing, and set logged next steps | Purchase pipeline | **Craig (Adviser)** | **2026-03-11** | Pipeline drift; missed conversions; delayed written business |</p><p>| Ensure active leads assumed “in touch” have **recent notes + clear next steps** (avoid unsupported statuses) | CRM discipline | **Craig (Adviser)** | **2026-03-11** | Weak audit trail; missed follow-ups; inflated engagement reporting |</p><p>| Confirm whether a **Protection First** is booked for **2026-03-05** and log details/notes | Protection pipeline | **Craig (Adviser)** | **2026-03-05** | Protection slippage; reduced ancillary revenue |</p><p>| Add **projected exchange date** into CRM and complete required reports | Ross Gibson &amp; Diane Morton | **Craig (Adviser) + Sean (Other)** | Not specified | Reporting inaccuracies; case progress delay |</p><p>| Obtain **deposit breakdown** evidence and update CRM with outstanding items | Emma McLaughlin | **Craig (Adviser)** | Not specified | Underwriting delay; potential offer slippage |</p><p>| Chase **NatWest** for offer document and log chase/next step in CRM | Christopher Patterson (NatWest